<!--
    nexus-0uuit (P1 — conexus-ddh0, cloud 5xx incident 2026-08-15): fixes the
    `topics.doc_count` recompute trigger's lock-order inversion that surfaced
    as `ERROR: deadlock detected` / SQLSTATE 40P01 -> HTTP 500 from
    `nexus.assign_from_chashes_<dim>`'s manifest write path.

    ROOT CAUSE (established via the cloud engine container log's deadlock
    DETAIL lines, all 8 events "while locking tuple in relation topics"):
    `nexus.topics_doc_count_recount_ins()` / `_del()` (taxonomy-003-doc-
    count-trigger.xml, AFTER INSERT/DELETE STATEMENT triggers on
    `topic_assignments` fired by every `assign_from_chashes_<dim>` call)
    UPDATE `nexus.topics` FROM an UNORDERED `SELECT DISTINCT tenant_id,
    topic_id FROM new_rows`/`old_rows` transition-table scan. Two concurrent
    callers whose affected-topic sets overlap lock those `topics` rows in
    PLAN order, not a shared deterministic order — A holds X wants Y, B
    holds Y wants X: a lock-order inversion of the trigger statement against
    itself, needing only ordinary write concurrency (assign_from_chashes is
    called on every flush's taxonomy hook).

    THE FIX, both parts prescribed in the bead (Hal ROOT CAUSE comment,
    2026-08-15 23:22):

    (1) CORRECTNESS — deterministic lock order FIRST: a `SELECT ... FROM
        nexus.topics WHERE (tenant_id, id) IN (...) ORDER BY tenant_id, id
        FOR UPDATE` acquires every affected topics row lock, in that fixed
        order, before any UPDATE touches them. An ORDER BY applied only to
        the driving subquery that then feeds an unordered join into the
        UPDATE's own FROM clause is NOT reliable (the UPDATE's internal join
        plan is free to visit rows in a different order); the ORDER BY has
        to sit on the FOR UPDATE query itself, whose row-locking happens as
        rows are returned from the (post-sort) top of the plan — the
        standard PostgreSQL "always lock rows in the same order" pattern.

        IMPLEMENTATION NOTE (found by TopicsDocCountDeadlockConcurrencyTest's
        GREEN phase against real PG, not merely reasoned about — THREE
        successive wrong/incomplete hypotheses below, kept as a record of
        what does NOT work, because every one of them looked correct by
        inspection):

          1. Bare `FOR UPDATE` (no `OF <table>` target list) is flatly
             rejected by PostgreSQL whenever a DISTINCT or GROUP BY clause
             appears ANYWHERE in that statement's FROM-list, even inside
             an unrelated joined subquery the lock target list never
             names — `ERROR: FOR UPDATE is not allowed with DISTINCT
             clause` / `... GROUP BY clause` (rewriting DISTINCT to an
             equivalent GROUP BY). CORRECTED (substantive-critic,
             2026-08-19, verified via direct PostgreSQL 16 EXPLAIN/query
             reproduction): an earlier version of this note also claimed
             `OF <table>` does not exempt a statement from this
             restriction — that claim is WRONG. `FOR UPDATE OF <table>`
             IS exempt: it compiles and runs even when the same
             statement's FROM-list joins a DISTINCT-producing subquery
             elsewhere. The bare-form restriction above is real and
             reproducible; the blanket extension to `OF <table>` was not.

          2. Materializing the DISTINCT pairs into plain arrays via
             `array_agg` in a genuinely separate, lock-clause-free
             statement, then `SELECT ... FROM nexus.topics t JOIN
             unnest(array1, array2) AS a(...) ON ... ORDER BY t.tenant_id,
             t.id FOR UPDATE OF t` DOES compile and run — and it was
             still abandoned: repeated runs against real PG
             (TopicsDocCountDeadlockConcurrencyTest, 17-20 real 40P01s
             out of 20 rounds, `Where: while locking tuple ... in
             relation "topics"` in the DETAIL) showed this form did NOT
             reliably serialize concurrent callers either. CORRECTED
             (substantive-critic, 2026-08-19): an earlier version of this
             note attributed that failure to a specific claimed
             PostgreSQL mechanism — "`LockRows` sits BELOW `Sort` for a
             JOIN plan" — which is WRONG (independent `EXPLAIN` on this
             exact unnest-join + `ORDER BY` + `FOR UPDATE OF t` shape
             shows `LockRows` sitting ABOVE `Sort`, i.e. receiving
             already-sorted input — the OPPOSITE of the claimed
             mechanism). The 17-20/20 deadlocks observed while testing
             this form are more likely attributable to CONTAMINATION from
             the SECOND, independent deadlock source documented below
             (the implicit FK-lock hazard in
             `assign_from_chashes_<dim>`'s own INSERT), which was present
             throughout every test run in this section and was never
             isolated out before this join-based form was rejected. The
             true mechanism behind this form's non-serialization (if one
             exists independent of source 2) was not conclusively
             established here. What IS established, empirically and
             repeatedly (see the trigger fix below): only the
             single-table, join-free, one-row-at-a-time `FOR UPDATE` loop
             reliably serializes concurrent callers under this test.

        THE TRIGGER-LEVEL FIX: a single-table, single-row, PK-indexed
        `SELECT ... WHERE tenant_id = ? AND id = ? FOR UPDATE` has no join
        and no sort/lock ordering ambiguity to defeat — Postgres locks
        EXACTLY the one named row. Running that statement inside a
        PL/pgSQL loop over a PRE-SORTED (`ORDER BY tenant_id, topic_id` in
        the `array_agg` itself) array of affected topics guarantees every
        concurrent invocation acquires the shared subset of topics locks
        in the identical relative order, one indexed row-lock statement at
        a time — the textbook deadlock-avoidance pattern, applied at the
        granularity where PostgreSQL actually honors it. This closes Hal's
        NAMED root cause exactly: the trigger statement no longer conflicts
        against ITSELF.

        CORRECTION (an earlier version of this note claimed "Verified GREEN
        ... 0 deadlocks" here — that was wrong; re-running
        TopicsDocCountDeadlockConcurrencyTest against real PG with ONLY this
        trigger fix applied still shows 20/20 rounds deadlocking, with the
        SAME "while locking tuple ... in relation topics" DETAIL, now
        pointing at THIS function's own `PERFORM ... FOR UPDATE` loop
        statement). The trigger fix is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT: a
        SECOND, independent deadlock source survives it. The INSERT inside
        `assign_from_chashes_<dim>`'s `persisted` CTE (taxonomy-006) carries
        an FK to `nexus.topics(id)`, and PostgreSQL takes an implicit `FOR
        KEY SHARE` lock on each REFERENCED topics row AS EACH inserted row
        is processed — in `nearest`'s own `ORDER BY c.chash, similarity,
        topic_id` order, which is uncorrelated with topic_id. Two concurrent
        callers touching an overlapping topic set therefore still acquire
        those implicit locks in arbitrary, per-call order — BEFORE the
        trigger's own perfectly-ordered loop even starts — and `FOR UPDATE`
        (requested by the trigger) conflicts with a `FOR KEY SHARE` already
        held by the OTHER transaction (from ITS own earlier, differently-
        ordered INSERT), reopening the identical two-party cycle one level
        up. No fix confined to the trigger function can close this: by the
        time the trigger runs, the conflicting lock from the earlier,
        unordered INSERT statement is already held. Reordering the INSERT
        itself (`... FROM nearest ORDER BY topic_id, chash` before the
        `persisted` CTE's INSERT) would close it at the root but touches
        `assign_from_chashes_<dim>` across all three dims (taxonomy-006) —
        deferred as separate, larger-blast-radius follow-up work, not
        bundled into this changeset. THE BELT THAT ACTUALLY CLOSES nexus-
        0uuit TODAY: `TaxonomyRepository#assignFromChashes` (Java) now wraps
        its transaction in `DeadlockRetry.run(...)`, the SAME bounded-retry
        belt `assignMany`/`importBatch` already use in this file (bead
        nexus-ps9wb) — the bead's own explicitly-authorized alternative
        ("retry the statement on SQLSTATE 40P01 with bounded backoff...
        after retries are exhausted"). Idempotent and safe: a deadlock
        victim's whole transaction is rolled back by Postgres before the
        retry re-runs, and `persisted`'s `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` makes a
        retried pass equivalent to the first. Verified GREEN (both fixes
        together, the SAME test): zero exceptions escape
        `assignFromChashes` across repeated concurrent rounds, and every
        topic's final `doc_count` matches an independent recount.

    (2) PROBABILITY — narrow the lock-holding window: the ORIGINAL body
        recomputes `doc_count` via a scalar `SELECT COUNT(*) ...` subquery
        evaluated once PER topics ROW inside the UPDATE's SET clause, so the
        table-scan work for topic N+1 happens while topic N's (and every
        earlier topic's) row lock is still held. This changeset restructures
        both functions as: (a) acquire ALL locks up front (the ORDER BY FOR
        UPDATE step above), then (b) recompute every affected topic's count
        via ONE batched GROUP BY aggregate (not a per-row correlated
        subquery), then (c) a single flat UPDATE ... FROM joining the
        pre-aggregated counts. The expensive scan work in step (b) still
        touches the same rows overall, but it happens as ONE pass rather
        than N re-evaluated subqueries feeding an UPDATE that is
        simultaneously trying to hold N locks — narrowing the window during
        which the just-acquired topics locks sit exposed to a concurrent
        acquirer.

        A true "increment by delta" was considered and REJECTED: `new_rows`
        (the INSERT transition table) captures BOTH genuinely new rows and
        rows touched only via `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` (the assign_from_
        chashes cross-collection pass) — Postgres transition tables do not
        expose xmin/xmax to distinguish the two, so a naive "+1 per
        new_rows entry" delta would silently inflate doc_count on every
        cross-collection re-flush of an already-assigned chash. The batched-
        aggregate recompute above stays an EXACT full recount (zero drift
        risk) while still achieving the bead's "narrow the lock window"
        goal by moving the scan cost off the per-row critical path.

    doc_count is STILL trigger-maintained only (taxonomy-003-4's COMMENT ON
    COLUMN contract, unchanged) — this changeset supersedes taxonomy-003-1/2
    (the function bodies) via CREATE OR REPLACE; taxonomy-003-3 (the trigger
    definitions themselves, `REFERENCING NEW/OLD TABLE AS new_rows/old_rows`)
    is untouched, so the transition-table names below match verbatim.

    NOT IN SCOPE (bead explicitly excludes): the write_many >30s Voyage-429
    edge-timeout 5xx class from the same incident window is a SEPARATE cause
    (CCE embedder retry-storm against the public edge's 30s upstream bound,
    tracked on conexus-ddh0 / a nexus-side CCE backoff bead) — this
    changeset does not touch it and must not be credited with fixing it.

    Test: TopicsDocCountDeadlockConcurrencyTest reproduces the deadlock against
    the PRE-fix function shape (two threads, overlapping topic sets in
    opposite orders, real PG) and proves the POST-fix function survives N
    concurrent rounds with correct final doc_counts.

    CORRECTION (nexus-6n51g, 2026-09-02 — MEASURED; do NOT act on the deferred
    prescription above). The reordering named above as what "would close it at
    the root" was implemented and measured, and it does NOT close it: 20
    concurrent rounds still produced 10 DeadlockRetry retries with the INSERT
    ordered by (topic_id, chash). It is neither necessary nor sufficient.

    The cause is lock STRENGTH, not lock ORDER. The implicit FK FOR KEY SHARE
    this header correctly identifies conflicts with exactly ONE row-lock mode:
    FOR UPDATE — the mode THIS changeset's own pre-lock loop requests. Both
    callers already hold KEY SHARE on the shared rows before either attempts
    the upgrade, so no insertion order can help. Since the trigger only ever
    UPDATEs doc_count, a NON-KEY column, FOR NO KEY UPDATE is the lock it
    actually needs: it does not conflict with FOR KEY SHARE, but still
    conflicts with itself, so this changeset's ordered one-row-at-a-time
    acquisition still serializes trigger against trigger and is preserved
    verbatim. Fixed in taxonomy-015-doc-count-lock-mode.xml, which carries the
    three measured variants.

    In short: this changeset closed source (1) and opened source (2) in one
    stroke, by taking the strongest lock rather than the sufficient one. The
    belt it added absorbed the result until it exhausted in production on
    2026-09-02.

    TIMING FOOTNOTE (conexus-13, 2026-09-02). The paragraph above this
    correction says the FK's KEY SHARE is taken "AS EACH inserted row is
    processed". That is wrong, and it matters only for REPRODUCING the
    deadlock, not for the diagnosis. PostgreSQL implements an immediate FK as
    an AFTER ROW trigger queued per row and fired at the END of the statement,
    so the RI checks take their locks in a tail immediately before the AFTER
    STATEMENT trigger — not interleaved with the inserts. The contention
    window is that tail. Measured consequence: two statements must end within
    it, which happens routinely in-process against a local PG (10-13 retries
    per 20 rounds) and essentially never across a WAN (nine natural variants
    against the live cloud, all zero on two independent instruments). Forcing
    the precondition reproduces it at once: 20/20 rounds on live 0.1.94.

-->
